{"date": "2026-10-20", "post_id": 1495526, "title": "A Year of Sunspots", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-20-an-anticrepuscular-rainbow-fan/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:\u00a0</strong>This is an unusual rainbow. For one thing, the rainbow is not complete, appearing only in a sunlit\u00a0<a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2e/Brise_Fan_LACMA_M.78.108.10.jpg\">fan</a>\u00a0-- but that by itself is not very unusual. What is most unusual is the fake location of\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/sun/\">the Sun</a>. The Sun appears to be toward the front, just below those hills -- but it is not. That is not\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.nesdis.noaa.gov/about/k-12-education/optical-phenomena/what-causes-rainbow\">how rainbows work</a>. Rainbows involve a backward\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reflection_(physics)\">reflection</a>\u00a0from falling\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.earthdata.nasa.gov/topics/atmosphere/rain\">rain</a>, so that the Sun must really be\u00a0<a href=\"https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:1400/1*t6nZgti5Q6Bp5Gm7zPK7vA.jpeg\">behind you</a>. In this case,\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2022-july-27-crepuscular-moon-rays-over-denmark/\">gaps between clouds</a>\u00a0near the\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-june-15-two-worlds-one-sun/\">setting Sun</a>\u00a0allowed rays to cross the entire sky and appear to\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2019-june-24-anticrepuscular-rays-converge-opposite-the-sun/\">converge in front</a>, an unusual phenomenon called\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-june-25-anticrepuscular-rays-over-sicily/\">anticrepuscular rays</a>. The photo was taken with a phone last week at sunset in\u00a0<a href=\"https://youtu.be/_KIIMuEaM-0?t=24\">Presenzano</a>,\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italy\">Italy</a>. The\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-february-4-anticrepuscular-rays-a-rainbow-fan-over-spain/\">seemingly reverse rainbow</a>\u00a0was noticed when\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.rmets.org/metmatters/ten-ways-dry-laundry-outside-all-year-round\">hanging up laundry</a>.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:\u00a0</strong>open space", "credit": "<a href=\"mailto:meryormella at gmail dot com\">Aurelia Degli Esposti</a>", "copyright": "<a href=\"mailto:meryormella at gmail dot com\">Aurelia Degli Esposti</a>", "alt": "Hills are seen in the distance below a partly cloudy sky. A group of orange rays appears to eminate from one area behind the hills. Across this fan of rays, near the top of the image, a short rainbow can be seen.  Please see the explanation for more detailed information.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-20-an-anticrepuscular-rainbow-fan/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/AntiRainbow_Esposti_5120.jpg?w=5120&h=3840&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 October 20 - A Year of Sunspots</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-20-an-anticrepuscular-rainbow-fan/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation:\u00a0This is an unusual rainbow. For one thing, the rainbow is not complete, appearing only in a sunlit\u00a0fan\u00a0-- but that by itself is not very unusual. What is most unusual is the fake location of\u00a0the Sun. The Sun appears to be toward the front, just below those hills -- but it is not. That is not\u00a0how rainbows work. Rainbows involve a backward\u00a0reflection\u00a0from falling\u00a0rain, so that the Sun must really be\u00a0behind you. In this case,\u00a0gaps between clouds\u00a0near the\u00a0setting Sun\u00a0allowed rays to cross the entire sky and appear to\u00a0converge in front, an unusual phenomenon called\u00a0anticrepuscular rays. The photo was taken with a phone last week at sunset in\u00a0Presenzano,\u00a0Italy. The\u00a0seemingly reverse rainbow\u00a0was noticed when\u00a0hanging up laundry.Tomorrow&#039;s picture:\u00a0open space\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"a year of sunspots\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"A Year of Sunspots\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-20-an-anticrepuscular-rainbow-fan/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/AntiRainbow_Esposti_5120.jpg?w=5120&#038;h=3840&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/AntiRainbow_Esposti_5120.jpg?w=5120&#038;h=3840&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"Hills are seen in the distance below a partly cloudy sky. A group of orange rays appears to eminate from one area behind the hills. Across this fan of rays, near the top of the image, a short rainbow can be seen.  Please see the explanation for more detailed information.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-10-20T00:05:00-04:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-10-19T13:16:20-04:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"A Year of Sunspots\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/AntiRainbow_Esposti_5120.jpg?w=5120&#038;h=3840&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 October 20<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/AntiRainbow_Esposti_5120.jpg\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/AntiRainbow_Esposti_5120.jpg\" alt=\"Hills are seen in the distance below a partly cloudy sky. A group of orange rays appears to eminate from one area behind the hills. Across this fan of rays, near the top of the image, a short rainbow can be seen.  Please see the explanation for more detailed information.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> A Year of Sunspots </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"mailto:meryormella at gmail dot com\">Aurelia Degli Esposti</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:\u00a0</strong>This is an unusual rainbow. For one thing, the rainbow is not complete, appearing only in a sunlit\u00a0<a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2e/Brise_Fan_LACMA_M.78.108.10.jpg\">fan</a>\u00a0-- but that by itself is not very unusual. What is most unusual is the fake location of\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/sun/\">the Sun</a>. The Sun appears to be toward the front, just below those hills -- but it is not. That is not\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.nesdis.noaa.gov/about/k-12-education/optical-phenomena/what-causes-rainbow\">how rainbows work</a>. Rainbows involve a backward\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reflection_(physics)\">reflection</a>\u00a0from falling\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.earthdata.nasa.gov/topics/atmosphere/rain\">rain</a>, so that the Sun must really be\u00a0<a href=\"https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:1400/1*t6nZgti5Q6Bp5Gm7zPK7vA.jpeg\">behind you</a>. In this case,\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2022-july-27-crepuscular-moon-rays-over-denmark/\">gaps between clouds</a>\u00a0near the\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-june-15-two-worlds-one-sun/\">setting Sun</a>\u00a0allowed rays to cross the entire sky and appear to\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2019-june-24-anticrepuscular-rays-converge-opposite-the-sun/\">converge in front</a>, an unusual phenomenon called\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-june-25-anticrepuscular-rays-over-sicily/\">anticrepuscular rays</a>. The photo was taken with a phone last week at sunset in\u00a0<a href=\"https://youtu.be/_KIIMuEaM-0?t=24\">Presenzano</a>,\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italy\">Italy</a>. The\u00a0<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-february-4-anticrepuscular-rays-a-rainbow-fan-over-spain/\">seemingly reverse rainbow</a>\u00a0was noticed when\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.rmets.org/metmatters/ten-ways-dry-laundry-outside-all-year-round\">hanging up laundry</a>.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:\u00a0</strong>open space\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-19-treble-clef-unusual-x-ray-hot-radio-source/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nRobert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti, Keighley Rockcliffe<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261020/html"}